{"name":"Machine Green","hex":"#a6a23f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"machine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A device that directs and controls energy, often in the form of movement or electricity, to produce a certain effect.","A vehicle operated mechanically, such as an automobile or an airplane."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make by machinery.","To shape or finish by machinery;  to shape subtractively by metal-cutting with machine-controlled toolpaths."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/machine"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Machine","blocks":[{"text":"A machine is a thermodynamic system that uses power to apply forces and control movement to perform an action. The term is commonly applied to artificial devices, such as those employing engines or motors, but also to natural biological macromolecules, such as molecular machines. Machines can be driven by animals and human power, by natural forces such as wind and water, and by chemical, thermal, or electrical power, and include a system of mechanisms that shape the actuator input to achieve a specific application of output forces and movement. They can also include computers and sensors that monitor performance and plan movement, often called mechanical systems."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Woman_operator_operating_milling_machine_-_DPLA_-_34dc4b36bfa1d73f44c4d6983b12f77c_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Woman_operator_operating_milling_machine_-_DPLA_-_34dc4b36bfa1d73f44c4d6983b12f77c_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine","wikidata":"Q11019"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Flint_hand_axe.JPG/500px-Flint_hand_axe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Flint_hand_axe.JPG/500px-Flint_hand_axe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A flint hand axe was found in Winchester.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlint_hand_axe.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/De_re_metallica_-_stamp_ore_crusher_%28125711574%29.jpg/500px-De_re_metallica_-_stamp_ore_crusher_%28125711574%29.jpg?u